      "body": "When tool schemas are passed to the response parser, an `integer` argument written as a whole-number float like `\"3.0\"` is now cast to `3` instead of being left as the raw string.\n\n- JSON Schema (draft 6 onward, including 2020-12) defines `integer` as any number with a zero fractional part, so `3.0` is a valid `integer` value. `jsonschema` (Draft 7 and 2020-12 validators) and Pydantic v2 both accept it, and Pydantic returns `3`.\n- Models do sometimes write integer arguments this way, and the tool then receives a string where its schema declares an integer.\n- Values with a fractional part (`\"3.5\"`), `inf` and `NaN` still fail the `integer` cast, so they fall through to the next declared type or stay raw, as before.\n- Adds a unit test next to the existing argument coercion tests.\n- Same change as in Transformers: https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/pull/49547.\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #38326623095](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38326623095)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #38326622989](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38326622989)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #38326623237](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38326623237)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->\n",
